  "claim": "Alexander Borodin wrote  the piece of music named after the Kiphchaks and Cumans that was performed by Cor Cois Abhann.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".\nPage: Cór Cois Abhann. Sentence: The choir is currently directed by Ian Sexton and has performed large scale works including Verdi's \"Messa da Requiem, Borodin's \"Polovtsian Dances\" and Duruflé's \"Requiem (Opus 9)\".",
