        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "0bedaf0c-d722-5b7b-9e94-e0f95fb447fe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Borodin, Ian Sexton, Giuseppe Verdi, Maurice Duruflé\n- Works: Stranger in Paradise, Polovtsian Dances, Prince Igor, Gliding Dance of the Maidens, Messa da Requiem, Requiem (Opus 9)\n- Organizations: Cór Cois Abhann\n- Concepts: Kipchaks, Cumans, Rus' people\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Borodin composed the opera Prince Igor, dated 1890 in this chunk.\n- The Polovtsian Dances form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera Prince Igor.\n- The melody of Stranger in Paradise was taken from Alexander Borodin's music, specifically Gliding Dance of the Maidens from the Polovtsian Dances in Prince Igor.\n- Polovtsian Dances is also spelled Polovetsian Dances.\n- The Russian title Половецкие пляски is transliterated as Polovetskie plyaski.\n- In this naming, Polovtsy was the Rus' people's name for the Kipchaks and Cumans.\n- Cór Cois Abhann is currently directed by Ian Sexton.\n- Cór Cois Abhann has performed large-scale works including Verdi's Messa da Requiem, Borodin's Polovtsian Dances, and Duruflé's Requiem (Opus 9).",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e639dff4-f153-55dd-8f60-4b22b349ab57",
        "text": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".\nPage: Cór Cois Abhann. Sentence: The choir is currently directed by Ian Sexton and has performed large scale works including Verdi's \"Messa da Requiem, Borodin's \"Polovtsian Dances\" and Duruflé's \"Requiem (Opus 9)\".",
